Bug #2: “Thread pool got saturated; entire service became unresponsive”
==================================================================================
What happened
Fanout was 6 in your toy example, but prod was 200–2000 (one request = many downstream calls).
 Under load, latency spiked, then timeouts, then cascading failures.

Why
    -Each work(taskId) is a blocking HTTP call.
    -With a fixed pool of 5/20/50 threads, blocking IO ties up threads.
    -Requests pile up behind the executor queue → queueing delay explodes.
    -Meanwhile your web server threads are waiting on allOf().join() → request threads also get stuck.

Classic symptom: p99 blows up, CPU low, lots of blocked threads.

Fix
Pick one (depending on your stack):
    -Bulkhead + bounded concurrency (limit fanout per request)
    -Use async HTTP client (non-blocking) and avoid blocking threads
    -In Java 21: use virtual threads for blocking IO fanout
    -Increase pool + bounded queue + backpressure (still risky)

Bound concurrency example (simple semaphore):

        Semaphore permits = new Semaphore(50); // max concurrent downstream calls
        CompletableFuture<ApiOutcome> f = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
            permits.acquireUninterruptibly();
            try { return callApi(id); }
            finally { permits.release(); }
        }, es);

