Bug #3: “Retry storm / duplicate updates (idempotency missing)”
==================================================================================
What happened

You fanned out to 10 services. One had a transient failure. You retried the entire fanout batch (or the whole upstream request was retried by gateway). Some services got the update twice.

Why

Fanout + retries without idempotency = duplicates.
Especially common if:

your upstream is at-least-once (Kafka consumer, webhook, etc.)

you retry on timeout (but downstream may have actually processed it)

Fix

Use idempotency key per downstream request (e.g., eventId + targetSystem)

Downstream stores “processed keys” or uses idempotent PUT semantics

Retry only failed targets, not the whole batch

Bug #4: “Success callback (thenAccept) did heavy work and slowed everything”
What happened

Each thenAccept did more than logging: DB writes, JSON parsing, metrics, maybe another API call.

Why

thenAccept runs by default on the thread that completes the previous stage.
So your precious IO threads (pool threads) finish HTTP call… and then spend time doing CPU/DB work, reducing throughput.

Fix

Offload post-processing:

.thenAcceptAsync(result -> persist(result), cpuExecutor)


Split executors:

ioExecutor for blocking HTTP

cpuExecutor for parsing/enrichment

dbExecutor for blocking DB (or use async DB)

Bug #5: “allOf().join() hung forever”
What happened

Some downstream calls never returned (socket hang, bad timeout config). Requests started piling up, and the service “froze”.

Why

No timeouts on the call, and no timeout on the future.
allOf().join() waits forever if any stage never completes.

Fix

Always enforce timeouts at 2 layers:

HTTP client timeouts (connect/read/request)

Future timeout (so your code completes even if HTTP client misbehaves)

Example:

CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> callApi(id), es)
  .orTimeout(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
  .exceptionally(ex -> fallbackOrMarkFailed(id, ex));

A solid “production story” you can tell (Order-entry flavored)

Situation: Order event needed fanout to 8 downstream systems (risk, compliance, allocations, confirmations, audit).
Problem: During peak, some downstream calls timed out. The code used exceptionally(... return null) and still returned success to the caller.
Operations thought orders were enriched, but compliance never received some updates.
Impact: Inconsistent state across systems; manual reconciliations.
Root cause: Exceptions swallowed + no global failure aggregation + thread pool saturation causing timeouts.
Fix: Bounded concurrency + per-target idempotency key + collect outcomes and fail/alert on partial failure + per-call timeout + async post-processing executor.
Result: p99 stabilized, partial failures visible, retries targeted, no duplicates.

